## 1. Keep the Existing Local Array

I would keep the existing local array because its indexes
correspond directly to the VM's stack slots.

The hash table would only be used to make name resolution
faster during compilation.

Conceptually, the compiler would contain:

```c
typedef struct {
  Local locals[UINT8_COUNT];
  int localCount;
  int scopeDepth;

  // Additional lookup structure:
  LocalNameTable localNames;
} Compiler;
```

Here, LocalNameTable represents a new hash-based lookup
structure designed for local variable names.

## 2. Resolve Variables Using a Hash Table

Instead of scanning all active locals, the compiler
would look up the identifier in the hash table.

Conceptual pseudocode:

```text
resolveLocal(name):
    if name exists in localNames:
        slot = localNames[name].top
        if locals[slot].depth == -1:
            report initializer error
        return slot

    return -1
```

An average hash table lookup takes approximately O(1)
time, making repeated variable resolution more efficient.

## 3. Preserve Variable Shadowing

The implementation must still support nested scopes.

For example:

```lox
{
  var a = "outer";
  {
    var a = "inner";
    print a;
  }
  print a;
}
```

The first print should use the inner variable, while the
second should use the outer variable.

To support this, each identifier can have a stack of
active local slot indexes.

When a new variable is declared, its slot is pushed onto
the list associated with that name.

When the scope ends, the corresponding slot is removed.

This restores the previously visible declaration.

## 4. Maintain Scope and Initialization Rules

The optimization must also preserve the rules described
in Chapter 22.

- Redeclaring a variable in the same local scope is an error.
- A variable in an inner scope may shadow an outer variable.
- Reading a local variable in its own initializer is an error.
- Local variables are discarded when their scope ends.

The compiler must continue to use the depth value -1
to identify variables that are declared but not yet
initialized.

The hash table should be updated whenever locals enter
or leave scope.

## 5. Performance Comparison

Original approach:
- Linear search through active locals
- O(n) worst-case resolution
- Simple implementation with little additional memory

Optimized approach:
- Hash table lookup by identifier
- Approximately O(1) average-case resolution
- Additional memory and bookkeeping required
- More complicated scope and shadowing management

## Is the Complexity Worth It?

I think this optimization could be useful in a language
that supports large numbers of local variables or requires
very fast compilation.

However, clox currently limits the number of locals in
scope to 256, so the linear search is already bounded.

Adding a hash table would make the compiler more complex
and require additional memory for tracking names and scopes.

Because the lookup happens during compilation rather
than runtime, the improvement would not directly make
the generated program execute faster.

For a small interpreter like clox, I would probably keep
the original array-based approach unless benchmarking
showed that local variable resolution was a bottleneck.
